SOLUTION
To delete the nautilus-ec2 instance while keeping the Terraform provisioning code intact, use Terraform's targeted destroy. 
This removes the real AWS resource and its state entry, but leaves the resource block in main.tf so you can re-provision it later.

📋 Steps
Check the resource address in your main.tf:

bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
grep -n "nautilus-ec2" main.tf
You'll see something like:

hcl
resource "aws_instance" "nautilus_ec2" {
  ...
  tags = {
    Name = "nautilus-ec2"
  }
}
Note the resource name (e.g., nautilus_ec2). If it's different, adjust the -target below.

Destroy only that instance:

bash
terraform destroy -target=aws_instance.nautilus_ec2 -auto-approve
Replace nautilus_ec2 with the actual resource name from your main.tf.

Terraform will:

Delete the EC2 instance in AWS.

Remove it from the Terraform state.

Keep the resource "aws_instance" ... block in main.tf untouched.

Verify the instance is terminated:

bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-instances \
  --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=nautilus-ec2" \
  --query "Reservations[].Instances[].State.Name"
Expected output:

text
[
    "terminated"
]
If the instance no longer appears at all (because it was fully removed), that also confirms deletion.

Confirm the code is still present:

bash
grep -n "nautilus-ec2" main.tf
The resource block should still be there.

💡 Why This Approach
terraform destroy -target=... deletes only the specified resource, not the whole configuration.

The main.tf file is not modified, so the provisioning code remains for future use.

The instance reaches the terminated state, satisfying the task requirement.

⚠️ If the Resource Is Not in State
