💡 Why -target and Not terraform destroy
Command	Effect
terraform destroy -target=aws_instance.ec2	Deletes only the instance; main.tf is unchanged ✅
terraform destroy	Destroys all resources and may prompt you to delete the config ❌
Deleting the block from main.tf	Removes the provisioning code ❌
The task explicitly requires you to keep the provisioning code, so targeted destroy is the correct approach.

⚠️ If Terraform Says the Resource Is Not in State
If the instance exists in AWS but isn't tracked by Terraform (e.g., created outside this config), import it first:

bash
# Get the instance ID
INSTANCE_ID=$(aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 ec2 describe-instances \
  --filters "Name=tag:Name,Values=nautilus-ec2" \
  --query "Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId" --output text)

# Import into state, then destroy
terraform import aws_instance.ec2 $INSTANCE_ID
terraform destroy -target=aws_instance.ec2 -auto-approve
✅ Final Check Before Submitting
bash
